Bug report:
[m1.2] N-v2: bump V2_UFN buffers 2048→4096 to fix self-compile SIGSEGV

After dfe0894a (S-A defensive backstop), parse_stmt_ir grew by 7 IR ops
at the call site of refuse_let_array_lit. Those extra ops pushed the
per-function V2_UFN_OPS[2048] over the silent-drop threshold during
self-compile. Dropped IR ops produced incomplete x86-64 → SIGSEGV in
stage1's compile_user_fn for the first user fn (v2_driver_context_new).

Bisect:
- pre-dfe0894a source self-compiles (md5=ccfbf10a…)
- post-dfe0894a source produces a binary (md5=f821f5…) that segfaults
  on its own self-compile (rc=139, no diagnostic — buffer-drop is silent)

Fix: bump V2_UFN_OPS / DST / SRC1 / SRC2 / IMM / LABEL_ID / BINOP_CODE /
FN_ID / RESULT_FCOUNT / STR_START / STR_END / FLOAT_SCALE all 2048→4096
plus the 26 `if idx < 2048` bounds checks in the ufn_record_* helpers.

Self-host fixed-point restored: stage1==stage2==stage3
(md5=c54005c7bfe951bdd1f5c0b8c185ee1b, 405,968 bytes).

Umbrella result (9/9 PASS):
  driver_self_compile ✓  science_spine ✓  f64_ladder ✓
  gum_primitives ✓       semantic_hardening ✓
  lean_single_fixed_point ✓  iso_budget ✓
  phase_j_conf_gate ✓    phase_y_gum_pbpk ✓

This restores all 5 native-compiler gates (driver_self_compile +
4 dependents) that had been failing rc=1 since dfe0894a.

