Bug report:
[codegen] Load scalar tuple elements into rax instead of leaving a pointer

Tuple field access `t.0` / `t.1` always materialized a copy and left rax
pointing at the copy, then set EXPR_TY=6 (struct-like). For scalar
elements (nslots=1) this meant i64 values printed as stack addresses
and comparisons compared pointers instead of values.

When the accessed element has nslots=1, emit `mov rax, [rax]` to
dereference and set EXPR_TY=1 (i64). Aggregates (nslots>1) keep the
materialize-and-return-pointer behavior.

Known limitation: f64 tuple arithmetic remains broken (EXPR_IS_F64 stays
0 because tuple element types aren't encoded in the current hash).
Printing f64 tuple elements works; arithmetic on them produces wrong
results. This was already broken prior to this commit; the failure mode
is different but no worse.

Gen2 == gen3 bit-identical (md5 12e53312...). Test suite 456/248 (+1 vs
prior): test_algorithms now passes; test_nelder_mead changed from
segfault (exit 139) to runtime error (exit 1).

